{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>It is not good that the man should be alone.</b> It does not appear to us that the man was created to be solitary in the world and not reproduce, for all created beings — male and female of all flesh — were created to establish seed, and also the grass and the trees have their seed within them. But it is possible to say that it was as the one who says (Berakhot 61a) that two faces (דוּ פַּרְצוּפִין) were created, and there came to be within them, by way of the organs of generation, a nature in which the male bestows upon the female a generative force, or you might say seed, according to the well-known dispute concerning conception. And the second face was a helper to the first in producing offspring. And HaKadosh Barukh Hu saw that it was good for the helper to stand opposite him, and that He would show it to him and separate it from him and join it to him according to His will — and this is what He said: \"I will make for him a helper opposite him.\" And the reason for \"it is not good\" is that it should not be said of him \"that it is good\" while he is alone, for it would not endure thus. In the account of Creation, \"good\" signifies permanence, as I explained in the passage \"and Elohim saw that it was good\" (Ramban on Genesis 1:4)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:23:43.764896060Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}